  date: "2026-09-03",
  body: `Prior Lessons — 1-Sentence Recap

Lesson 023: Multiple patterning extends resolution by splitting one logical layer into several physical patterning steps, trading simplicity for more cost, cycle time and overlay risk.

Lesson 024: EUV dose, source power and stochastic defects connect pattern quality directly to scanner wafers-per-hour and effective lithography capacity.

Lesson 025: EUV availability converts installed scanners into productive capacity, so uptime and service can matter almost as much as tool count.

Lesson 026: High-NA EUV improves resolution by raising numerical aperture, but its value depends on whether it removes more manufacturing complexity than it introduces.

Lesson 027: EUV pellicles protect reusable reticles from repeating contamination defects, but their transmission and thermal behavior can affect scanner throughput.

The big idea

The word **mask** makes an EUV reticle sound like a simple stencil.

It is not.

At 13.5 nm, EUV light is absorbed so strongly by ordinary materials that the mask cannot work like the transparent masks used in conventional optical lithography.

Instead, an EUV mask is a **precision multilayer mirror with a patterned absorber on top**.

The scanner shines EUV light onto the mask at an angle. Regions without absorber reflect useful light back into the projection optics; absorber-covered regions suppress that reflection and create the circuit pattern.

So the basic structure is:

low-expansion substrate → multilayer mirror → protective cap → patterned absorber

That stack has to be almost unbelievably uniform, because the same reticle is reused across many fields and wafers.

## 1. Why EUV needs a mirror instead of transparent glass

Visible light can pass through glass. EUV generally cannot.

At a 13.5 nm wavelength, even materials that look transparent to us absorb EUV strongly.

That is why EUV scanners use reflective optics throughout the light path rather than ordinary transmissive lenses.

The mask follows the same rule.

Instead of asking light to pass through selected regions, the EUV reticle controls **where light is reflected**.

This is the first mental shift:

**DUV-style mask intuition is transmission; EUV mask intuition is reflection.**

## 2. One metal surface is not reflective enough

A normal metal mirror is still not good enough at EUV wavelengths.

The solution is a multilayer reflector made from many alternating nanometer-scale layers, typically molybdenum and silicon.

Current EUV mask stacks are commonly described as roughly **40–50 Mo/Si layer pairs**, with the complete reflective stack around a few hundred nanometers thick.

Each individual interface reflects only part of the incoming EUV light.

But engineers tune the layer thicknesses so those tiny reflections return in phase and reinforce each other through constructive interference.

Think of many people pushing a swing at exactly the right moment. Each push is small, but coordinated pushes add together.

The result is a mirror optimized around 13.5 nm rather than a surface that reflects broadly across visible wavelengths.

## 3. Even the optimized mirror loses a lot of light

A good Mo/Si EUV multilayer reflects only roughly the **high-60% to around-70%** range near its design wavelength, not close to 100%.

That matters because the mask is only one reflective surface in a scanner that already loses photons at other mirrors.

Every lost photon makes the source, resist and exposure-time problem harder.

This connects directly to Lesson 024:

lower optical efficiency → less useful EUV reaches the resist → more source power or exposure time may be needed → scanner throughput can fall

